{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TrDKVBBvxTEEWnMHB1tmwE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fnru9BsuNLWquhFjja51J2","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","subagent_type":"claude","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations\"\n\nThe paper studies how linguistic ambiguity in policy specifications causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Qwen3-32B-AWQ, Qwen3-8B), and 2,400 episodes in τ²-bench, the paper finds:\n- C1: +31pp violation rate increase from ambiguity (95% CI [27,35], p<2.3e-8), all 6 types significant\n- C2: No significant type differentiation (Cramér's V=0.15, omnibus p=0.17) → \"binary sufficiency\" thesis\n- C3: Suggestive visibility effect (CP vs scopal, Δ=-7.2pp, p=0.09, direction consistent across 4 models)\n- C4: Model×Type interaction not significant (p=0.14), but descriptive pattern of capability-dependent type sensitivity\n- Error analysis: 4 failure modes, 44% CP traces show conflict detection vs 8% scopal\n- Reliability: ICC=0.68, test-retest band ±7.5pp\n- Realism: 4/5 linguistic properties match DRIP-R (parse depth significant at p=0.04)\n- Classifier PoC: DeBERTa-v3-large, 82% accuracy, F1=0.80\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper covers most planned experiments:\n- plan_001: 300 clauses (50/type × 6 types), quality pass 92% ✓\n- plan_002: API model experiments (1200 episodes) ✓\n- plan_003: Local model experiments (1200 episodes) ✓  \n- plan_004: Full statistical analysis (binary effect, type uniformity, visibility, interaction) ✓\n- plan_005: Error analysis (300 traces, 4 failure modes) ✓\n- plan_006: Reliability test (720 episodes, ICC=0.68) ✓\n- plan_007: Realism validation (KS tests against DRIP-R) ✓\n- plan_008: Classifier PoC (DeBERTa, 82% acc) ✓\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the following dimensions. Be rigorous and calibrated to EMNLP 2026 standards (21% acceptance rate for main conference).\n\n### 1. Soundness of experimental design\n- Is the matched-pair causal design well-executed? Any confounds?\n- Are 50 clause pairs per type sufficient for the claims made?\n- Is τ²-bench appropriate for this study?\n- Is cross-judge evaluation (κ=0.78) adequate?\n\n### 2. Statistical analysis\n- Are the statistical tests appropriate (McNemar, omnibus χ², Fisher's exact, logistic regression)?\n- Is the pre-committed V<0.20 threshold for C2 well-justified? Could it be too lenient?\n- Is the interpretation of the \"informative null\" (C2) honest given V rose from 0.08→0.15?\n- Is reporting C3 at p=0.09 appropriate? How should it be framed?\n\n### 3. Reliability and validity\n- ICC=0.68 is \"moderate-to-good\" — is this sufficient for the claims?\n- Test-retest band ±7.5pp vs effect size +31pp — does this adequately address noise concerns?\n- Realism validation: 4/5 properties match but parse depth differs (p=0.04) — is this a concern?\n\n### 4. Completeness / missing experiments\n- Compare what's reported vs the experiment plan. Note any gaps.\n- The unambiguous baseline rates are suspiciously flat within each model (GPT-5.4: all 11%, GPT-4.1: all 13%, etc.) — is this reported/discussed?\n- Is the classifier PoC (§7 Discussion) appropriate in scope, or does it feel bolted on?\n\n### 5. Reproducibility\n- Are enough details provided to reproduce the experiments?\n- Is the code/data release mentioned?\n\n### 6. Weaknesses (bullet list, ordered by severity)\n\n### 7. Strengths (bullet list)\n\n### 8. Scores\n- Soundness: 1-5\n- Excitement: 1-5  \n- Reproducibility: 1-5\n- Overall Assessment: use the ARR scale (1-5, half-point increments)\n  - 5 = Award, 4 = Conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Resubmit, 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nFormat your output as a structured review. Be specific with line references to tables/sections. Do NOT be generous — calibrate to the 21% acceptance bar.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29970,"cache_read_input_tokens":23507,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29970},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f464be2-bed5-4578-9852-e291b31c81e7","uuid":"ac672bff-a452-4992-a562-6369d8570457"}
