{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TrDKVBBvxTEEWnMHB1tmwE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FpbNjh31j9kRcWuNBNKqS5","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","subagent_type":"claude","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations\"\n\nThe paper studies how linguistic ambiguity in policy specifications causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Qwen3-32B-AWQ, Qwen3-8B), and 2,400 episodes in τ²-bench, the paper finds:\n- C1: +31pp violation rate increase from ambiguity (95% CI [27,35], p<2.3e-8), all 6 types significant\n- C2: No significant type differentiation (Cramér's V=0.15, omnibus p=0.17) → \"binary sufficiency\" thesis\n- C3: Suggestive visibility effect (CP vs scopal, Δ=-7.2pp, p=0.09, consistent across 4 models)\n- C4: Model×Type interaction not significant (p=0.14)\n- Error analysis: 4 failure modes, 44% CP traces show conflict detection vs 8% scopal\n- Reliability: ICC=0.68, test-retest band ±7.5pp\n- Realism: 4/5 linguistic properties match DRIP-R\n- Classifier PoC: DeBERTa-v3-large, 82% accuracy\n\nKey claims:\n1. All policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents (title claim)\n2. Binary detection suffices for safety triage (no need for type-specific pipelines)\n3. Visibility moderates risk (explicit conflicts → more agent awareness → fewer violations)\n\n## Related Work cited\n- Berry & Kamsties 2003 (nocuous/innocuous ambiguity in RE)\n- Li et al. 2024 (11-type NLP ambiguity taxonomy)\n- Massey et al. 2014 (RE ambiguity framework)\n- Chantree et al. 2006 (coordination ambiguity detection)\n- Wasow et al. 2005 (ambiguity vs vagueness)\n- Ruan et al. 2024 (LM agent risk identification)\n- Zhan et al. 2024 (indirect prompt injection in agents)\n- Murthy et al. 2024 (DRIP-R: retail policy decision quality)\n- Yao et al. 2024 (τ²-bench)\n- Wu et al. 2024 (SEAL/PolicyBank)\n- Wang et al. 2024 (PhantomPolicy)\n- Shafran et al. 2024 (policy formalization)\n- Frazier 1979 (garden-path sentences)\n- Kennedy 2007 (vagueness/gradable adjectives)\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the following dimensions. Be rigorous and calibrated to EMNLP 2026 standards (21% acceptance rate).\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- Is the research question genuinely novel? Has anyone studied ambiguity type → agent safety violations before?\n- Is the \"binary sufficiency\" thesis a genuine contribution or a consequence of insufficient power/type coverage?\n- Is the matched-pair causal design novel in this context?\n- How novel is the visibility hypothesis connecting psycholinguistics to agent safety?\n- Does the paper advance understanding beyond what DRIP-R (Murthy et al., 2024) already established?\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n- Are there important missing references? Consider:\n  - Broader agent safety literature (e.g., alignment, RLHF safety)\n  - Policy compliance / specification testing\n  - Ambiguity resolution in NLP (disambiguation systems)\n  - Psycholinguistics beyond Frazier 1979 (e.g., Ferreira & Patson 2007 on good-enough processing)\n  - Human-AI interaction / instruction following\n- Is the positioning against DRIP-R fair and accurate?\n- Is the taxonomy (§3) well-grounded in prior work?\n\n### 3. Contribution Significance\n- Is the +31pp effect size surprising or expected? Would the community learn something non-obvious?\n- Is \"all ambiguity is nocuous\" (the title claim) well-supported or overstated given only 6 types, 2 domains?\n- Does the \"binary sufficiency\" thesis have real practical value, or is it trivially implied by the null result?\n- What is the practical impact for the NLP/agent safety community?\n\n### 4. Framing and Narrative\n- Is the paper well-motivated?\n- Is the connection to RE literature (Berry & Kamsties) natural or forced?\n- Is the psycholinguistic connection (garden-path, visibility) convincing or speculative?\n- Is the title appropriate? (\"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous\" — does the data support \"all\"?)\n\n### 5. Writing Quality\n- Is the paper clearly written?\n- Are the claims well-calibrated to the evidence?\n- Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n\n### 6. Weaknesses (bullet list, ordered by severity)\n\n### 7. Strengths (bullet list)\n\n### 8. Scores\n- Soundness: 1-5\n- Excitement: 1-5\n- Reproducibility: 1-5\n- Overall Assessment: use the ARR scale (1-5, half-point increments)\n  - 5 = Award, 4 = Conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Resubmit, 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nFormat your output as a structured review. Be specific. Do NOT be generous — calibrate to the 21% acceptance bar.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29970,"cache_read_input_tokens":23507,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29970},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f464be2-bed5-4578-9852-e291b31c81e7","uuid":"78fa648d-3eec-4596-99e0-2f079cdf8c05"}
