{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VaLe7joT9dnsoc3b7HJdWd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VzUjSYXRTE5hfL5CnR45fF","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","subagent_type":"claude","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations\"\n\nThe paper studies how linguistic ambiguity in policy specifications causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Qwen3-32B-AWQ, Qwen3-8B), and 2,400 episodes in τ²-bench, the paper finds:\n- C1: +31pp violation rate increase from ambiguity (95% CI [27,35], p<2.3e-8), all 6 types significant\n- C2: No significant type differentiation (Cramér's V=0.15, omnibus p=0.17) → \"binary sufficiency\" thesis\n- C3: Suggestive visibility effect (CP vs scopal, Δ=-7.2pp, p=0.09, consistent across 4 models)\n- C4: Model×Type interaction not significant (p=0.14)\n- Error analysis: 4 failure modes, 44% CP traces show conflict detection vs 8% scopal\n- Reliability: ICC=0.68, test-retest band ±7.5pp\n- Realism: 4/5 linguistic properties match DRIP-R\n- Classifier PoC: DeBERTa-v3-large, 82% accuracy\n\nKey claims:\n1. All policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents (title claim)\n2. Binary detection suffices for safety triage (no need for type-specific pipelines)\n3. Visibility moderates risk (explicit conflicts → more agent awareness → fewer violations)\n\n## Related Work cited\n- Berry & Kamsties 2003 (nocuous/innocuous ambiguity in RE)\n- Li et al. 2024 (11-type NLP ambiguity taxonomy)\n- Massey et al. 2014 (RE ambiguity framework)\n- Chantree et al. 2006 (coordination ambiguity detection)\n- Wasow et al. 2005 (ambiguity vs vagueness)\n- Ruan et al. 2024 (LM agent risk identification)\n- Zhan et al. 2024 (indirect prompt injection in agents)\n- Murthy et al. 2024 (DRIP-R: retail policy decision quality)\n- Yao et al. 2024 (τ²-bench)\n- Wu et al. 2024 (SEAL/PolicyBank)\n- Wang et al. 2024 (PhantomPolicy)\n- Shafran et al. 2024 (policy formalization)\n- Frazier 1979 (garden-path sentences)\n- Kennedy 2007 (vagueness/gradable adjectives)\n\n## Important Observations\n- Table 2/10: Unambiguous violation rates are IDENTICAL across all 6 types within each model (GPT-5.4=11% for all, GPT-4.1=13% for all, etc.). This is very unusual for real experimental data.\n- The paper reports 7 pages of main content + 1 page appendix. References are reasonable.\n- The paper does NOT mention code/data release.\n- Some references use \"and 1 others\" format which is unusual.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the following dimensions rigorously, calibrated to EMNLP 2026 standards (21% acceptance rate).\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- Is the research question genuinely novel? Has anyone studied ambiguity type → agent safety violations before?\n- Is the \"binary sufficiency\" thesis a genuine contribution or a consequence of insufficient power/type coverage?\n- Is the matched-pair causal design novel in this context?\n- How novel is the visibility hypothesis connecting psycholinguistics to agent safety?\n- Does the paper advance understanding beyond DRIP-R?\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n- Are there important missing references? Consider:\n  - Broader agent safety/alignment literature\n  - Policy compliance / specification testing\n  - Ambiguity resolution in NLP\n  - Psycholinguistics beyond Frazier 1979 (good-enough processing, Ferreira & Patson 2007, etc.)\n  - Human-AI interaction / instruction following\n- Is the positioning against DRIP-R fair?\n- Is the 6-type taxonomy well-grounded?\n\n### 3. Contribution Significance\n- Is the +31pp effect size surprising or expected?\n- Is \"all ambiguity is nocuous\" well-supported or overstated (only 6 types, 2 domains)?\n- Does \"binary sufficiency\" have practical value or is it trivially implied by the null?\n- What is the practical impact?\n\n### 4. Framing and Narrative\n- Is the RE connection natural or forced?\n- Is the psycholinguistic connection convincing or speculative?\n- Is the title appropriate given the evidence?\n\n### 5. Writing Quality\n- Clarity, claim calibration, any overclaiming\n\n### 6. Provide: Weaknesses (ordered by severity), Strengths, and Scores\n- Soundness: 1-5\n- Excitement: 1-5\n- Reproducibility: 1-5\n- Overall Assessment: ARR scale (1-5, half-point increments): 5=Award, 4=Conference, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit, 1=Do not resubmit\n\nOutput a structured review. Be specific. Do NOT be generous.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3856,"cache_read_input_tokens":53477,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3856},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f464be2-bed5-4578-9852-e291b31c81e7","uuid":"ba78c9ce-376d-4ba1-b42a-4e21643fcf95"}
